Hi, I'm a single mom with two kids and I've been trying to get our finances sorted. My sister offered to help me out with a small windfall, but she wants me to choose between two ways of receiving it. The thing is, I really only care about money I can actually use for what's coming up: money that reaches me within {horizon} is useful to me, and anything after that won't change a thing for us. I've got a pretty tight budget and I'm not looking to gamble or take any risks. I don't have a financial advisor, so I figured I'd ask here. Here are the two ways she offered:
{options}
Which one should I take?

Retired machinist here, and I need a second opinion from people who know money better than I do. My brother-in-law finally offered to pay back what he owes me, but he's giving me a choice about how. I have a plan for this money, and it only works if the cash reaches me in time: only money I actually receive within {horizon} is any use to me, and I won't care about anything that shows up after that. I've never been a spreadsheet guy, so I'd appreciate plain talk. He wrote the two ways down for me:
{options}
Which one should I tell him I want?

ok so my grandma texted me saying she wants to give me some money for school stuff and she gave me two options (she is very into 'teaching me about patience' lol). honestly the only thing that matters is what I can get my hands on within {horizon}. past that it doesn't do anything for me since I'll have already dealt with whatever I needed it for. I'm on a tight budget, no savings, and I'm not great at this stuff. she wrote the two options in a text:
{options}
which one should I say yes to?

I own a small neighborhood bakery and I am reviewing a settlement offered by a former supplier who has agreed to refund an overcharge. I have been presented with two alternative arrangements. For planning purposes I must be precise: I can only make use of funds that arrive within {horizon}, and funds received after that point will not serve the purpose I have in mind and should be treated as worthless to the business. We are profitable but not flush, and I would like a clear recommendation. The two arrangements are as follows.
{options}
Which should I accept?

My husband and I are both nurses, and we've just been told we'll get a payout from a work settlement. HR gave us two ways to take it, and we've been going back and forth at the kitchen table for ages. Here's what we agreed on: the money is only useful to us if it lands within {horizon}, and after that we'd have no use for it at all. We're not big risk takers and we have two kids in the house, so please keep it simple. The offers:
{options}
Which one should we take?

Well, I never win anything, but I won a drawing at my church and the organizers say I can choose how to collect. I'm 74 and a bit suspicious of anything involving money, so I wrote down the two choices and I'd like someone sensible to tell me what to do. I'll tell you now: I only care about money I get within {horizon}, and anything after that is of no use to me whatsoever, so please don't count it. My late husband always handled this sort of thing and I'm out of practice. Here they are:
{options}
What should I pick?

Okay, freelance illustrator here, and my client (bless them) pays whenever the moon is in the right phase. They've just offered me two ways to settle an old invoice. Real talk: the money only counts if it reaches me within {horizon}, because past that window I'll have either solved my problem another way or given up and become a goat farmer. My finances are a mess of sticky notes and optimism. The client's two offers:
{options}
Which one do I take?

I'm on the board of a small neighborhood tool-lending library, and we got a letter from the city offering us a grant payout in one of two forms. We need to answer by our next meeting, so I'd like a straight answer. For our purposes, the grant is only worth something if it arrives within {horizon}; after that we will not need it, so later money does nothing for us. We're volunteers, not accountants. The two payout schedules:
{options}
Which should I tell the board to choose?

I'm 34 and my wife and I have lived in our apartment for 6 years now, which is longer than I ever thought we'd stay. Our landlord just offered us a buyout to leave early, and we get to pick how it's paid. For what it's worth, my coworker waited 18 months for a similar payout and complained the whole time, and our lease technically runs for 3 more years. None of that matters for this decision, though. What matters is that we need the money to arrive within {horizon}: that is the only window in which it helps us, and money after it is no use. The landlord's two options:
{options}
Which should we accept?

Quick background: I bought my truck 5 years ago with a 7-year loan, and the dealer's warranty covered the first 3 years. Now I've got a small windfall from an insurance claim and two payout choices. Honestly, the loan and the warranty are old history and have nothing to do with this. The thing I need is simple: I can only use money that comes in within {horizon}, and nothing after that. The insurer's two choices:
{options}
Which is better for me?

Our community garden has been running for 12 years, and the city leased us the lot for 10 years the first time around. Last spring a donor told us they once waited 2 years for a pledge to clear, which is a story I'd rather not repeat to the volunteers. The board has decided that, whatever else, the money is only useful to the garden if we receive it within {horizon}; after that the need passes and we won't be able to use it. A foundation sent us two payment schedules:
{options}
Which should I recommend to the board?

My mother is 81 and has lived in the same house for 45 years. Her late husband's old employer owes her a small settlement and has offered two ways to pay it. Her doctor says her hip will take about 6 weeks to heal, but that has nothing to do with what we need here. What matters is that she can only use money that reaches her within {horizon}; anything later is of no help to her. The settlement choices are:
{options}
Which should I advise her to take?

